how you a  little bit how  this principle can can help us  and before i do this i think at least a  few of you  uh work in a very different field and  quantum computing so i just wanted to  have one slide to give some essential  information so that the rest  makes a bit more sense  i guess this is at the cost of  saying what a qubit is  uh to those of you who already work in  quantum  so uh you know as you have heard a qubit  is the quantum analog of a bit it can be  in a superposition of zero and 1 here  alpha and beta are complex numbers and  really you should think of this as a  two-dimensional vector  where the standard bases vectors  are 0 and 1. so you can think of 0 as  pointing  upwards and 1 is pointing to the right  and any linear combination of the 2 is a  valid cubit state so for example we  could have  1 over square root of 2 0 plus 1 over  square root of 2 1 this is sort of the  vector pointing to the 45 degree  angle we could have the same state with  a minus sign this would correspond to  minus 45 degree  and one one really important thing is  that really the only way to extract  classical information from a quantum  status via a measurement  so you're allowed to ask the question is  the qubit state zero or one and you will  get an answer if you do so a zero or one  depending on the  the probabilities will depend on the  coefficients alpha and beta now the  crucial difference for quantum  information is that upon measurement the  state will collapse to the outcome that  you observe so you no longer have the  state that you started with this is very  important  and of course you know  you can ask the same question is it zero  one with respect to any other bases  there's nothing special about the the  standard bases and the post-measurement  state will be one of the two orthogonal  basis factors  and so let's see how we can leverage all  of this  so let's consider uh the task of key  distribution  so in key distribution we have two  parties alice and bob they have a simple  goal which is to agree on a uniformly  random secret key this is a secret in  the sense that it's known only to the  two of them  and they would like to do this by  communicating over a public  communication channel this is public in  the sense that any malicious  eavesdropper you can read everything  that passes through the channel  intercept any message  so if you stop and think about this for  a moment you you'll find that it's very  difficult to come up with a key  distribution protocol if you can  intercept  everything  in fact it turns out that it's  impossible classically to realize a key  distribution with unconditional security  so what i mean here by unconditional  security is that the security should be  information theoretic so it should hold  even  against the computationally unbounded  disrupter  and this uh this impossibility is quite  easy to see you know any bit that alice  sends to bob and vice versa can be  intercepted  by eve  like so  and so eventually at the end of any  protocol even bob will have seen exactly  the same transcript so if bob was  supposed to learn a key then so  information theoretically so can  now things are different in the quantum  setting but alice is not restricted to  to just sending bits she can send qubits  for example she could choose to send  qubits in the standard bases or in the  45 minus 45 degree bases but you know  let's say that she chooses to send  a bunch of qubits  and for each qubit she chooses uniformly  at random between these four possible  states  now of course  eve can still intercept each qubit on  the way from alice to bob  and she can try to learn some  information about it okay but the point  is that eve does not know the basis  choice that alice made and so with at  least half probability eve will be  measuring in an incorrect basis and this  measurement as a result will disturb the  state that eventually gets the ball  and so the key is that you know in a  later step alice and bob can reveal to  each other their basis choices  and they can detect with high  probability if eve has tampered with a  significant  fraction of the communication  [Music]  and if so they they will abort the  protocol so so this is really the key  idea here and using this idea  one uh can realize key distribution  quantumly with unconditional security  and this is uh something that we already  knew in 1984  from a protocol of bennett in versailles  so so there is this start contrast  between the quantum and the classical  setting  for key distribution and the natural  question is can we go beyond key  distribution  and uh unfortunately the answer seems to  be no and this is because there there is  another  primitive called bit commitment  which we know to be provably impossible  to realize  with unconditional security even using  quantum information  and unfortunately bit commitment is a  fairly basic primitive in cryptography  it is implied by many other more complex  primitives  and so we seem to be a little bit  out of luck here  and i think it's fair to say that  for for several years over many years  quantum cryptography has focused more on  the study of key distribution in various  seconds  and so what i want to highlight  today is  so the line of research has emerged or  gained traction a bit more recently  which i find  most interesting which is the following  so you know in in almost all of  classical cryptography  or almost all of it is based on  computational hardness assumptions  what i mean by this is that the security  of  almost all cryptographic protocols is  not unconditional but it's based on the  assumed hardness of some computational  problem like for example  computing a discrete logarithm or  factoring  and so why not try to combine quantum  information with computational hardness  in the hope of getting interesting  cryptographic primitives not with  unconditional security  but at least from weaker assumptions  than is possible classically  so let me just state briefly one  one example of this from a recent work  so the primitive is multi-party  computation which is  more powerful than key distribution  it allows actually mutually distrustful  parties to compute an arbitrary function  of their joint inputs securely so this  is very powerful  now classically we know how to build  this based on  various computational assumptions about  specific problems but crucially we do  not know how to build this from one way  functions  so just to remind you a one-way function  is a function that is easy  to compute what is hard to invert on  average  and the existence of one wave functions  is pretty much the minimal uh assumption  that we know to be useful for for  anything in cryptography so ideally we  would like to reduce you know all of our  primitives to one-way functions but this  does not seem to be  the case for multi-party computation in  fact there is a black box sort of  impossibility here  however it turns out that maybe  surprisingly that in the quantum world  we can actually reduce multi-party  computation all the way to the existence  of one way function  y functions and this is due to two  recent concurrent results  so i guess i have one last comment which  is you know in this case multi-party  computation is something that we already  knew how to build just you know  classically just from stronger  assumptions so this is a you know i  would say more of a qualitative  uh result  however there are very interesting  examples where the combination of  computational hardness and quantum  information can really lead to new  primitives that are even out of reach of  what is possible classically  i think cheaper and later we'll talk  about some of this  so  [Music]  so you said  that uh one-way functions imply quantum  multi-party communication are those is  that like one-way functions that are  secure to you know quantum inverters or  yeah  let's say one more question

